# Step 9 — Install the global `boukensha` command

This step packages Boukensha as a Python application. After installation, you
can start the REPL by typing `boukensha` from any directory.

## Build and install

Open a terminal outside any active virtual environment and install it for your
user. Ubuntu provides the interpreter as `python3`, not `python`:

```sh
cd ~/projects/claude-code-camp-2026-Q2/week1_baseline/python/09_global_executable
python3 -m pip install --user --force-reinstall .
```

This installs without `sudo`. Verify the command:

```sh
boukensha --version
boukensha doctor
```

The expected version is `boukensha 0.9.0`.

If your prompt starts with `(.venv)`, either run `deactivate` before the
user-level installation above, or install only into that virtual environment:

```sh
python -m pip install --force-reinstall .
```

Do not combine `--user` with an active virtual environment: its user
site-packages are intentionally hidden.

If Ubuntu rejects the user install as an “externally managed environment,”
install the application with `pipx`:

```sh
sudo apt install pipx
pipx ensurepath
pipx install --force .
```

Open a new terminal after `pipx ensurepath`, or follow the PATH instructions it
prints.

If the command is not found, obtain Python's user base and add its `bin`
directory to your shell PATH:

```sh
python3 -m site --user-base
export PATH="$(python3 -m site --user-base)/bin:$PATH"
```

Put the `export` line in `~/.bashrc` or `~/.zshrc` to preserve it for future
terminals. `boukensha doctor` also reports the actual executable directory and
whether it is currently on PATH.

## Configure Boukensha

Boukensha reads `~/.boukensharc`. For this repository:

```yaml
boukensha_dir: ~/projects/claude-code-camp-2026-Q2/.boukensha
```

`boukensha_dir` contains `.env`, `settings.yaml`, and optional prompt
overrides. `BOUKENSHA_DIR` is the equivalent one-command override and takes
precedence over the rc file:

```sh
BOUKENSHA_DIR=~/projects/claude-code-camp-2026-Q2/.boukensha boukensha
```

The installed distribution includes a working implementation. During lesson
development, `boukensha_path` can select another Python snapshot:

```yaml
boukensha_dir: ~/projects/claude-code-camp-2026-Q2/.boukensha
boukensha_path: ~/projects/claude-code-camp-2026-Q2/week1_baseline/python/09_global_executable
```

| Purpose | Environment variable | rc setting | Default |
|---|---|---|---|
| Runtime configuration | `BOUKENSHA_DIR` | `boukensha_dir` | `~/.boukensha` |
| Python implementation | `BOUKENSHA_PATH` | `boukensha_path` | bundled package |

The implementation path must contain `boukensha/__init__.py`; it is unrelated
to the shell PATH.

## Diagnose and run

```sh
boukensha doctor
boukensha
```

Doctor reports the installed and selected paths, Python and Boukensha
versions, provider, model, and credential readiness. It never prints a
credential value. Supported environment keys are `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`,
`OPENAI_API_KEY`, `GEMINI_API_KEY`, and `OLLAMA_API_KEY` for Ollama Cloud.
Local Ollama needs no key.

Inside the REPL, `/help` lists all commands. `/clear` clears conversation
history, and `/exit` or `/quit` leaves the session.

Command-line forms:

```text
boukensha
boukensha doctor
boukensha --version
boukensha --help
```

After rebuilding, reinstall with `--force-reinstall` and use `boukensha
doctor` to confirm which implementation and executable are active.
